            <ArticleTitle>Carboxyhemoglobin levels in patients with cocaine-related chest pain.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>147-50</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Carboxyhemoglobin (COHb) levels were measured in patients who came to an emergency department complaining of acute chest pain. For subjects not receiving prior oxygen therapy, those with cocaine-related chest pain (n = 10) had a higher mean COHb level than a comparison group (n = 28) with nonischemic chest pain (4.50 +/- 2.40 vs 2.73 +/- 0.66; p &lt; 0.05). Four of the seven (57 percent) who smoked crack had COHb levels greater than 4.5 percent, while only one of six (17 percent) smokers of only tobacco had such a level. These findings suggest an additional mechanism, the formation of COHb, which could aggravate cocaine-induced cardiotoxicity.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
